      {
        "id": "gfQmy1DhHXEex_aW",
        "title": "Text 8",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>4. Graph</strong></u></p><p>The graph tool is meant to be used for graphing equations and number relationships. You can change the axes of the graph independently, so the lines of the grid are not always square. </p><p><em>Create a new graph in your workspace under Initial Challenge. </em>Try representing number relationships in different ways by: </p><ol><li>Add points by hand by clicking <em>Add points by hand </em>on the Graph tool tray.<em> </em>As you click on the graph, the points connect to show that you are forming a mathematical relationship between the points.  Hint: You can remove points by clicking <em>Select, </em>then highlighting individual points and clicking delete. The lines will reconnect. Select multiple points with shift-click.</li><li>Plot data from a table. Select <em>Add Data </em>and add your dinosaur height and length data to the graph. Hint: You can change which part of the table gets graphed on each axes in the graph tile. Make sure you&#x27;re graphing length and height!</li></ol>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "j5lDeYPxqZbK5M3K",
        "title": "Text 12",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>5. Graph Comparison</strong></u></p><p><em>Drag the dog table into your workspace under Initial Challenge. </em></p><p>Add the dog data to both your coordinate grid and your graph tile</p><p><em>Drag the text tile below into your Initial Challenge workspace and answer the questions.</em></p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      [
        {
          "id": "9_TYCCVpvkGGfb0f",
          "content": {
            "type": "Table",
            "columnWidths": {
              "7I4wpDDvOzntLXJv": 80,
              "LE48l49RLgU4BN7H": 80
            }
          }
        },
        {
          "id": "J7iMz2NbeSJpVW8c",
          "title": "Text 14",
          "content": {
            "type": "Text",
            "format": "html",
            "text": [
              "<p>What can you do with the dog in both graphs? </p><p>Try selecting different parts of the dog in each graph--points, lines, and the whole animal. Then test the different graph types. Record what you find out. Try answering these questions: </p><ol><li>What can you move with the mouse, and what won&#x27;t move?</li><li>Can you duplicate the figure (make a copy)?</li><li>If you can make a copy, try doing things with the copy.</li><li>Can you move it? Rotate it?  Stretch it?</li><li>Can you measure a length of one segment?</li></ol><p>See if you can <em>unlink</em> the graph. What happens?</p>"
            ]
          }
        }
      ],
      {
        "id": "MnyCKDneBCuVLjhB",
        "title": "Text 15",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>6. Bar Graph</strong></u></p><p>A Bar Graph is used to represent frequency, or the number of times something appears. </p><p>The table below show&#x27;s a class survey where everyone reported their favorite animal and their age.</p><p><em>Drag the Class Survey table from below into your workspace under the Initial Challenge heading. Drag over a new bar graph tile and add the Class Survey Data to it.</em></p><ol><li>Look at the bar graph when it is sorted by favorite animal and then change it to be sorted by age</li><li>Change the x-axis label to see the difference between age and favorite animal.</li><li>Change the color of the bars</li></ol>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "xXLIQFbIFoASdZoJ",
        "content": {
          "type": "Table",
          "columnWidths": {
            "SyaogF1-7mD15qTE": 167,
            "Lo41zEIrLSuysOw6": 80
          }
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "N6lpQezNOBqmPhkc",
        "title": "Text 9",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>7. Sketch</strong></u></p><p>This simple draw tool can help you explain your answers when nothing else will. </p><p><em>Add a new Sketch tool into the Initial Challenge workspace. Play with it and draw a picture while doing the following:</em></p><ol><li> Change the color</li><li> Add shapes </li><li> Flip, align, group, and rotate the shapes</li><li> Use the freehand tool</li><li> select multiple shapes by clicking and dragging the mouse over them. Group them together</li><li> Add a stamp</li><li> Add text explaining your picture</li></ol><p>Hint: You can hide some of your shapes by clicking on the Show/Sort panel on the left-hand side of the sketch tile. Every item you add to the tile gets listed here. You can click on an item in the show/short list to selects it in the sketch. You can select multiple items with a shift-click. You can show or hide items clicking on the eye icon or drag an item in the list using the waffle to change its level.  </p><p>You can also use the navigator in the bottom right hand corner (unless you drag it to the top) to adjust the viewing area.</p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "U21EQQG9O0P2LgrE",
        "title": "Features of Sketch",
        "content": {
          "type": "Image",
          "url": "cmpclue/images/demo.jpg",
          "filename": "cmpclue/images/demo.jpg"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "Tk-rjujAaXdD7cry",
        "title": "Text 10",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>8. Equation</strong></u></p><p>The Equation tool is for typing mathematical expressions and equations. </p><ul><li>Use &quot;+&quot;, &quot;-&quot;, and &quot;*&quot; on your keyboard to add, subtract and multiply. </li><li>Click the divide symbol in the tool bar to divide.  </li><li>To write a fraction type the numerator, then &quot;/&quot;, and then the denominator. </li><li>For a mixed fraction, click the mixed fraction icon in the tool bar.</li><li>Use &quot;^&quot; to create an exponent.</li><li>Use the tab key, arrow keys, or the mouse to move between the different parts of the expression.</li><li>You can change the label at the left to describe your mathematical expression.</li></ul><p><em>Create a new equation tile under Initial Challenge in your workspace. Enter the equation shown in the picture below , then edit it, and name it your favorite animal.</em></p>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "JbTA4jddq8MofD_k",
        "title": "Equation to Copy",
        "content": {
          "type": "Image",
          "url": "cmpclue/images/eq.jpg",
          "filename": "cmpclue/images/eq.jpg"
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "dSAk4GvDuVLc6NkN",
        "title": "Text 11",
        "content": {
          "type": "Text",
          "format": "html",
          "text": [
            "<p><u><strong>9. Number Line</strong></u></p><p>The Number Line tool it can help us understand how mathematical operations work, particularly problems involving negative numbers. The Number Line below shows the answer to 2 - 5 where the &quot;-&quot; sign indicates counting 5 units to the left. The endpoints can be changed.</p><p><em>Copy the Number Line below into your workspace under the Initial Challenge heading and </em></p><ol><li>Change the end points to be something other than -5 and 5. </li><li>Add one closed point anywhere on the number line</li><li> Add one open point anywhere on the number line</li></ol>"
          ]
        }
      },
      {
